# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — what is written first: vision, requirement, or use case

## Answer

On my rules, the order within one pass is **vision, then requirements, then use cases**, and it is a strict direction, not a menu: 「勝兵先勝而後求戰，敗兵先戰而後求勝」 — the winning army first makes victory certain and only then seeks battle (my #2, quoted). Use cases are the seeking of battle; the vision and requirements are the 先勝. Across passes it is a **loop**: probing with cases sends a missed behaviour back into the requirement (my #17), and a requirement change that alters who the product is for, or what it will not do, sends it back into the vision. The loop closes when a whole pass adds no new fixed unit and no new "fails if", and every "fails if" already has a check shown failing once on a planted wrong input — that is a comparison, not an invented number. EJ's "use cases first" holds as **discovery before the requirement is frozen**, where the case list is a probe and its cases are evidence, never the count; it breaks as **definition**, where the case list sets coverage (my #12, #13, #15).

Hmm, that answer is a bit long but "one or two sentences" — I'll compress. Let me write 2-3 sentences.

## How I decided

1. Lay the whole war out before fighting it. My #1 is an algorithm: five matters, seven comparisons, and the ordered chain 地生度，度生量，量生數，數生稱，稱生勝 — ground gives measurement, measurement quantity, quantity number, number weighing, weighing victory (my #1, quoted). The ground is the product situation; the first of the three artifacts that can carry it is the vision (who it is for, the problem, what it will not do, how we know it works). So vision is the first term, not the cases. My reading of the mapping; the rule gives the direction, the mapping is mine.

2. Prefer the whole to the broken: 上兵伐謀，其次伐交，其次伐兵，其下攻城 — the highest use of troops strikes plans, the lowest attacks walled cities (my #3, quoted). A vision is the plan level; a use case is the city. Spend first where the leverage is.

3. Make yourself unbeatable first: 先為不可勝，以待敵之可勝 (my #2, quoted). A requirement with a level, a "fails if", a check and a named decider is the "unbeatable" half; the unknown user behaviour is the enemy's half. Cases are how you wait for the enemy to show form, not how you build your own half.

4. Know yourself before the other side: 知彼知己，百戰不殆 (my #4, quoted); knowing your own troops can attack but not whether the ground permits battle is 勝之半也. Requirements are the self-knowledge; use cases are the other side. Half a victory is what you get if you generate cases over a requirement you have not fixed.

5. Mix harm into benefit before you commit: 智者之慮，必雜於利害 (my #6, quoted). This is why each requirement must carry a "fails if" and a check that can fail: the harm column is written before the benefit. The trial's clause with no "fails if" is exactly the harm left out.

6. Learn the enemy by acting on him: 策之而知得失之計，作之而知動靜之理，形之而知死生之地，角之而知有餘不足之處 (my #17, quoted). This is the one place "use cases first" is right: a case list run against the requirement is 角之, testing in contact, and the trial shows it surfaced a clause with no "fails if" and a "fails if" with no test and no code. But the list is a probe; its output is a repair to the requirement, not a count.

7. Do not demand of the people what the 勢 must supply: 善戰者，求之於勢，不責於人 (my #12, quoted). Both engines' raw coverage moved with the prompt (46% vs 20%) while coverage over the fixed units was 80% both times. The count belongs to the units, the cases are the people; the same 80% from 28 and from 200 cases is my evidence for that.

8. Not every road is taken: 途有所不由 (my #13, quoted). Use cases cannot be exhaustive, and an attempt to make them exhaustive is the general's fault of 愛民，可煩也 — loving the people, he can be harried (my #15, quoted). EJ's 80%-of-units target is the accepted loss; the price is named in costs.

9. The ground decides the form, so name it first: on scattered ground do not fight; on dead ground, fight (my #19, quoted). If the product already exists with old use cases, you are on a different ground and the loop starts from the old cases; if it is greenfield, there is nothing to probe and the vision carries the whole first pass. That is why my answer is conditional.

10. Stop when it no longer accords with advantage: 合於利而動，不合於利而止 (my #5, quoted). This is what closes the loop: a pass that no longer changes the requirement's units has stopped according with advantage.

