## What I need to know
(conditional)

1. Who is the vision's maker — EJ, or the user/customer the product is for? If it is the customer, then the vision check is "every line traces to the customer's words" and EJ's role is the decider, not the author; my #11 says foreknowledge must come from the people who know the situation, and the person the product is for is that person.
2. Does the product already exist with old use cases? If yes, the first pass starts from them (my #19, #17), and "vision first" only means the vision is named before the requirement is changed; if no, the first pass is vision → requirement → cases.
3. What does "checked" mean here — who is allowed to fail a check? If EJ is the only one who can fail the vision check, the loop's speed is EJ's availability; if any named decider can, it is faster.
4. Is the 80% target per requirement or per product? If per product, a requirement with a real gap can hide under another requirement's coverage; if per requirement, it cannot, and the loop can close per requirement.
5. Is a requirement's fixed-unit set allowed to change during a pass? If not, the loop cannot close except by luck; if yes, the close condition (no new unit, no new "fails if") is the one I gave.
